.\" patch.1 -- maux's patch(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text, written against src/bin/patch.s
.\" and its six body modules), so it carries no third-party notice
.\" -- MIT like the code, same posture as env.1/man.1/tsort.1.
.TH PATCH 1 "September 17, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
patch \- apply a diff to a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B patch
[
.B \-b
] [
.B \-R
] [
.BI \-p " num"
] [
.BI \-i " patchfile"
] [
.BI \-o " outfile"
] [
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Patch
reads a difference listing \(em what
.BR diff (1)
writes \(em and applies it to a file, which is how a change made
somewhere else arrives here.
.PP
The listing is read from
.I patchfile
if
.B \-i
is given and from the standard input otherwise.  Its FORM is
detected, never declared: all three of
.BR diff 's
output forms are understood, the unified one
.RB ( "@@ \-l,s +l,s @@" ),
the context one
.RB ( "*** l,l ****" " /" " \-\-\- l,l \-\-\-\-" )
and the normal one
.RB ( 3c3 ", " 3a4 ", " "3,5d2" ).
Text before the first hunk is skipped, so a patch that arrived
inside a mail message still applies.
.PP
The file to patch is the
.I file
operand if one is given.  Otherwise it is taken from the listing's
own header lines \(em the old name first, then the new one, the
first of the two that can be opened.
.PP
Each hunk is matched EXACTLY: every one of its context and
deleted lines must equal, whole line for whole line, a run of
lines in the file.  Where that run sits is guessed from the line
number the hunk states, corrected by the lines earlier hunks have
already added or removed; if the guess misses, the run is searched
for outward from it.  A hunk that matches nowhere is REJECTED: the
file keeps its original text there, and the hunk is written,
verbatim and in the form it arrived in, to
.IR file .rej.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Save the original, unpatched contents as
.IR file .orig
before writing anything.  Without it nothing is saved.
.TP
.B \-R
Apply the listing BACKWARDS, as though its two files had been
given to
.I diff
in the other order \(em which is how a change is undone.
.TP
.BI \-p " num"
Delete
.I num
leading pathname components from every pathname the listing's
header names, so that a patch made in another directory still
names a file here.
.TP
.BI \-i " patchfile"
Read the listing from
.I patchfile
instead of the standard input.
.TP
.BI \-o " outfile"
Write the patched result to
.I outfile
and leave the original file untouched.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/PATCH  this page
.PP
.PD 0
.TP 16
.IR file .orig
the original contents, with
.B \-b
.TP 16
.IR file .rej
the hunks that could not be applied
.PD
.SH "SEE ALSO"
diff(1), ed(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 if every hunk applied, 1 if one or more hunks
were rejected, and 2 for an error \(em a usage error, a patch or
target that cannot be read, a listing with no hunk in it, a
malformed hunk, or an input past the limits in BUGS.  Progress and
failure messages go to standard error, not standard output: the
two reference implementations word them differently, so nothing
here should be parsed for them.
.SH BUGS
Capacity is fixed at build time and an input past it is REFUSED,
never silently truncated: 250 lines and 12000 bytes of text in the
file being patched, 120 lines and 2400 bytes in any ONE hunk.
.PP
A derived name must still be a legal ProDOS name \(em 15
characters including the extension, enforced by the pathname
parser rather than at creation \(em so a base name longer than 11
characters is TRUNCATED to 11 before
.B .orig
or
.B .rej
is appended.
.PP
One file per invocation.  A listing that names several files
(what
.B "diff \-r"
would produce, which this
.I diff
cannot) applies only its first.
.PP
There is no FUZZ factor and no
.B \-l
(match ignoring white space): a context line that differs by so
much as a blank makes its hunk fail.  There is no
.B \-N
either, so a patch that has already been applied is not
recognised as such \(em its hunks simply fail and are rejected.
.PP
Every line written ends in a newline, including the last, so
patching a file whose final line had none adds one.
